  <div class="container hero-inner">
    <p class="label reveal" style="color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Positioning · Yardstick</p>
    <h1 class="display-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="color:#fff;max-width:880px;">Credibility and persuasive power: the yardstick for everything else</h1>
    <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.88);max-width:720px;margin-top:1rem;">Any machine can sound plausible these days. Being believed is something else.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">There is a difference between a sentence that sounds right and a sentence that is believed. The first can now be produced in seconds — language models effortlessly generate phrasing that seems fluent, correct and appropriate. That is exactly why fluent phrasing is losing its value: it no longer proves anything.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Credibility arises another way. It is not a property of sentences but a consequence of decisions about content — and those cannot be automated, because someone has to stand by them.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What credibility consists of</h2>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">Verifiability</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A figure you can check carries more weight than any superlative. On the Delikato salad range, the fat content is printed on the pack variety by variety — from 4 % for tzatziki to 13 % for tuna salad. The range's promise is thus not claimed but quantified, including where the figure is less flattering. That is precisely what makes it credible: information that appears only when it is favourable is advertising. Information that always appears is a statement of fact.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Verifiable does not necessarily mean quantifiable, though. It is enough that the reader can check the claim against their own situation — at the very moment they read it.</p>

  <figure class="reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin:2.5rem auto;max-width:620px;">
    <img src="/img/volksbank-lippstadt-luftbild-2011.webp" alt="Full-page newspaper advertisement for Volksbank Lippstadt: aerial photo of the town with the branch locations marked, headline in German: “Of course you can bank online with Volksbank. But you can also just walk round the corner.”" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="1769" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
    <figcaption style="font-size:.75rem;color:rgba(var(--ink-rgb),.5);margin-top:.6rem;line-height:1.5;">Volksbank Lippstadt, daily newspaper advertisement, 2011. Aerial photo: digital orthophoto, flight of 27.04.2008. Data source: State of NRW / Geobasis NRW, <a href="https://www.govdata.de/dl-de/by-2-0" rel="license noopener" target="_blank" style="color:inherit;">dl-de/by-2-0</a>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A regional bank has no technical lead over direct banks — online banking is much the same everywhere. Its only advantage that cannot be copied is its proximity to the customer. The sentence “we are there for you locally”, however, appears in every other bank advertisement and is read accordingly: as a platitude.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">So the advertisement does not claim proximity; it reveals it: an aerial photo of the town with every branch in it. Whoever opens the paper looks for their own street first — and answers the question of distance themselves before the bank has said anything about it. An argument the reader completes themselves does not have to be believed; they know it.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">In passing, the advertisement turns the weaker position around. Not “we can do online too” — that would be an apology — but: everyone can do online, only here can you walk into a branch. The work lies in that decision, not in the design.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">Consistency over time</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A brand that claims something different every year has no statement, only a history of statements. Every change devalues the previous one — and because it usually comes with a new presence, it also devalues the recognition built up. That is the economic core of the principle <a href="/en/positioning/concept-before-execution/">change follows strategy</a>: whoever changes rarely can rely on what they said before.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">Omission</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What you do not claim supports what you do claim. Most brand presences fail not from too little but from too much: every additional promise dilutes the others, and a single untenable one is enough to cast doubt on all the rest.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Omission is therefore not restraint but an investment — and it is the most uncomfortable part of the work, because someone in the company is attached to every point that is cut.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Persuasive power is not being liked</h2>
  <div class="compare-grid reveal reveal-delay-1">
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">Being liked</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Needs approval. Is measured in the approval committee: does everyone agree?</p>
      <p class="compare-text">A draft that everyone agrees with has typically lost every edge on which a decision could have caught. That is no coincidence — edges are exactly where objections arise.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">Persuasion</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Needs a counterpart, a claim and a risk. Is measured in the market: does someone change their behaviour?</p>
      <p class="compare-text">Persuasion presupposes that you could be wrong. Whoever risks nothing says nothing worth contradicting — and so nothing worth agreeing with either.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-top:2rem;">Three things follow operationally: <strong>a line of argument instead of a list</strong> — a thought that develops rather than a list of equally weighted points. <strong>One thought per carrier</strong> — an advertisement, a side of a pack, a slide carries exactly one statement. <strong>Dramaturgy instead of completeness</strong> — the order decides whether something lands, not the quantity.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The opposite of being liked is not sobriety</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">This point is regularly misunderstood, by agencies too: arguing against being liked is not arguing against feeling. In many markets emotion is not the decoration but the product — in confectionery, in professional sport, in fashion, in food. Communication that triggers no emotion there has failed its task, not fulfilled it.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The line runs elsewhere:</p>

  <div class="compare-grid reveal reveal-delay-1">
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">Borrowed emotion</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">It comes from the image, not from the subject: a sleeping child, a family in backlight, a sunrise. The feeling is real, but it belongs to no one.</p>
      <p class="compare-text">The test: swap the sender. If the advertisement works equally well for a bank, an insurer and an energy supplier, the feeling was borrowed — and borrowed feelings do not pay into a brand.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">Earned emotion</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">It arises from the facts themselves. The aerial photo above creates a feeling — recognition, reassurance, a small surprise — but only after the reader has found their own street.</p>
      <p class="compare-text">This feeling cannot be transferred to another sender, because it is tied to the sender's concrete situation. That is exactly what makes it an asset rather than a mood.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-top:2rem;">Producing emotion has become cheap — a warm image and a warm sentence are available in minutes, in any quantity, from anyone. What has not become cheap: deciding <em>which</em> emotion carries with <em>whom</em> at the moment of decision, whether the product backs it up, and whether it still holds after the purchase. The decision about the emotion is the service, not its production.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 4rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What this says about the use of AI</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">In this work, artificial intelligence is a good tool and a poor yardstick. It speeds up research, variants, checks and production — and it is now the first line of defence when drafts are cleared, even before the law firm.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What it cannot do is decide what is left out. Omission requires accepting a loss that nobody demands — and taking responsibility for it. A system optimised to give a helpful answer will rarely conclude that the best measure is not to do something.</p>
